\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133467 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133467 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n![](images/f1/f1906279bd2a08c55aad73b8974edcccfdd3b156c5d471d185e2edc61e538528.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc780837-b763-5ba9-981a-2373afcb524a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133467 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](images/f7/f7bdc5e767fbd22592a2b9bf8e51517ad6927e7edc495fd7a6ce73b7ae29e4a1.svg)\nAs a result, $|A| = 0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so \n\n$$\n|B| = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row: \n![](images/c1/c1211b127ba1daa7863aef3501287f93fda48791ba3a072b98b777bd54ff31d7.svg)\nAs a result, $|C| = 0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133468 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133468 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b3/b36961dd90e73504bf46d63827d1ea68292c01a7990f8fa3eb00eee62a4b1813.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero column\n\nII. $A$ has proportional rows\n\nIII. $A$ has identical columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33ca37e5-c598-52a2-861f-2d5bfa6fd571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133468 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n![](images/ce/ce6418b20309f4fdf0fdde343eef9fd6fdaa547d40207382c512537c5e509296.svg)\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](images/cd/cdfd4bbe379b1e1c68412fa70c67e4ba9e355a329bb8f457ecb17523755f2ea1.svg)\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133911 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Two Proportional Rows/Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133912 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8aef330a-81b3-56c4-9559-67df673e8687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133913 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134042 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fc007430-4cc9-562b-9192-4d3c9dccd500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $2det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcb38d39-eeab-5f94-bc9b-9cb1d6df2c04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134395 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(A)=1\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=4\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has two identical rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2det(A)+4det(B)=2\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134901 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134901 #edb/ref 1061 41745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134901 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b68428f5-f5bb-5090-b7f9-4971dc931914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134901 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134902 #edb/ref 1061 41746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e105e6ae-64b8-5812-85e8-b5c3ef7b6c7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134903 #edb/ref 1061 41747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2a27f52-c605-5d01-84c1-49531f59d922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134904 #edb/ref 1061 41750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23e26677-bdeb-5a45-a6d3-4a8d02a72732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134905 #edb/ref 1061 41751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93111f5e-5fd2-5a5b-81b1-27969a9b5d1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134906 #edb/ref 1061 41754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4aec093b-050b-5263-9ef2-c875455b8c07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134907 #edb/ref 1061 41755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81571aa7-ce3d-5713-bf67-a49538fee0ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134908 #edb/ref 1061 41766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c5dbeea-b2e4-5fe9-9d8c-98750164b70c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135151 #edb/ref 1064 "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n![](images/6b/6bb8b64f7ed8730e641cdf88fc6fe7add40bc15061095c6b1f57c1b764b28feb.svg)\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+2}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+3}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135151 #edb/ref 1065 6888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135151 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135152 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n![](images/74/7477781b618ec384b1638d01707acf545e7d9882ffca5bbc0efde4e224b04308.svg)\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135152 #edb/ref 1065 6889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6813d49e-3908-5735-8196-8e1aad5a14e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135152 #edb/ref 1067 "Determinants with Identical Rows or Identical Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135153 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n![](images/81/8181d36ff1378310744b0d74f42f2934eb1e41053f45ecfeacf0c813abe994df.svg)\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135153 #edb/ref 1065 6890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135153 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe83e8c9-948a-596f-a2d9-c2ae2fe4f335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135153 #edb/ref 1067 "Determinants with Proportional Rows/Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135154 #edb/ref 1064 "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero row/column\n\nII. $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\nIII. $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\nIV. $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135154 #edb/ref 1065 6891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135154 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9d8b0323-170c-5a60-b372-1a258c4848cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135154 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135155 #edb/ref 1064 "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n![](images/6e/6e046341b7a1b8f6760797f7db89a7e021a6ace6b5cdf28bd5217998cb450837.svg)\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n![](images/f5/f593c123eb081da0399e74c665325774758b48c5f086a7475b959905e1de2763.svg)\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n*$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135155 #edb/ref 1065 6893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135155 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f7191c6-60ca-5c44-befc-8dbad7779b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135155 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of a Zero Determinant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269436 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269436 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269436 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1321a890-eb6a-54ac-bf48-1d4c77ff2d9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269584 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2417ace3-b1df-55bb-9e6e-f0cf724a7d7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269889 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269889 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "445c99d0-1565-5d0f-bd0a-b81957ffc9f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270902 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8d5f2c0-248a-5010-b938-a0307212261a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271495 #edb/ref 1080 "-10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271495 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271495 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe1fdb0e-edd1-53d9-ac51-6c3cbff27cc4" #edb/ref 175